Z||000000||KRMFiero||09-01-2001||09:59 AM||||I installed my Rally guages into my 86 se, when i turn the key on the voltage goes to where it should but the oil pressure guage benches over (I dont even know if there is a sending unit there, half the engine isnt there) but the oil pressure guage on the Tach stays at 0 (I havent put my 120 cluster in yet cause the tach is being recalibrated for 8 cyl)<P>Kyle||||reg||5
Z||000001||Raydar||09-01-2001||10:05 AM||||The oil pressure gauge is the cause of the problem. Mine did the same thing. <BR>BTW... In order to get the V-6 tach to read correctly in my 88 coupe, I just removed the tach circuit board from the 88 tach, and installed it on the V-6 SE tach. If you look at the tachs carefully, the sweep is the same arc, the V-6 tach just continues to a higher RPM.<BR>I was a bit leery of this at first, but it seems to read correctly. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroJoe||09-01-2001||10:38 PM||||I just added 120 gauges and the aux gauges to my 87 coupe.  Tach reads exactly the same, really easy to swap the tach board. Besides a few voltmeter warning light issues I had, caused by my own modifications, everything looks great and reads perfect.||||reg||1
